= Shortcut learning — Technical =

== Decision-rule framing ==
A shortcut is a decision rule that minimizes source-distribution risk using cues that do not support the intended invariance or transfer behavior.

A model can implement multiple rules simultaneously, so shortcut use need not imply absence of core-feature learning.

== Detection ==
Approaches include:

* counterfactual interventions;
* background/context swaps;
* subgroup evaluation;
* attribution/explanation analysis;
* environment-specific testing;
* feature ablation;
* causal probes.

Each method has assumptions and can produce false reassurance.

== Mitigation ==
Data balancing, augmentation, regularization, invariant objectives, representation constraints, and explicit environment variation can reduce shortcut use.

Recent work shows that mitigation methods can also suppress useful features, so success should be evaluated behaviorally rather than assumed from the method.

